# Predicting CFTR Missense Variant Pathogenicity with ESM-2

A bioinformatics project investigating whether a protein language model
can distinguish pathogenic CFTR missense mutations from benign variants
using sequence information alone.

This project was completed as part of the Genes and Genomics module at
Imperial College London.

## Overview

Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR protein, which functions
as a phosphorylation-regulated ATP-gated anion channel.

In this project, I used Meta AI's ESM-2 protein language model to estimate
the functional impact of CFTR missense variants.

For each mutation, the wild-type residue was masked and ESM-2 was used to
calculate the probability of the wild-type and mutant amino acids.

Variant effect was quantified using the log-likelihood ratio:

LLR = log P(mutant | sequence context) - log P(wild-type | sequence context)

More negative LLR values indicate substitutions considered less compatible
with the sequence context and therefore potentially more damaging.

## Dataset

Clinically characterised CFTR missense variants were obtained from CFTR2
and ClinVar and grouped into:

- Benign
- Class II — folding / trafficking defects
- Class III — gating defects
- Class IV — conductance defects

## Methods

- Protein language model: ESM-2 (`facebook/esm2_t33_650M_UR50D`)
- Framework: PyTorch / Hugging Face Transformers
- Variant scoring: masked-language-model log-likelihood ratio
- Statistical analysis:
  - Welch's t-test
  - Tukey HSD
  - descriptive statistics
- Visualisation: Python / Matplotlib / Seaborn

## Results

ESM-2 broadly distinguished benign CFTR variants from pathogenic
Class II and Class III variants.

Mean LLR:

| Variant class | Mean LLR |
|---|---:|
| Benign | -3.37 |
| Class II | -8.42 |
| Class III | -7.65 |
| Class IV | -2.77 |

Class II and III mutations showed substantially more negative scores,
consistent with substitutions occurring at strongly constrained protein
positions.

